HiSilicon Hi3798CV200 AVSP program
=================================

The histb-avsp.bin file is the 17,920-byte AVS processor program used by
the Hi3798CV200 VDH/SCD.  It is stored as little-endian 32-bit words and
has the following SHA-256 digest:

  4ec0ea0b21ebd45057bbad20a1b69893fc02a4ae6244f46503781141fc785d3f

The image was reconstructed from the product-linked g_avsp_firmware
object.  The following independently shipped CV200 product trees contain
byte-identical 4,480-word objects:

  - HiSTBAndroidV600R002C00SPC030, HD_FULL
  - HiSTBLinuxV100R005C00SPC050, LNX_CFG 32-bit and 64-bit
  - HiSTBLinuxV100R005C00SPC060, LNX_CFG 32-bit and 64-bit
  - the CV200 32-bit Linux 4.4 BSP

The product source paths are:

  source/msp/drv/vfmw/vfmw_v5.0/firmware/product/Hi3798CV200/
    HD_FULL/*/driver/HiSCDV200/scd_drv.S
  source/msp/drv/vfmw/vfmw_v5.0/product/Hi3798CV200/
    LNX_CFG/{32bit,64bit}/core/driver/HiSCDV200/scd_drv.S
  source/tee/drv/vfmw/vfmw_v5.0/core/driver/HiDSPV100/
    dsp_v100_avsp.h

The product build links g_avsp_firmware from scd_drv.S and explicitly
sizes it to 17,920 bytes.  The HiDSPV100 header compiles to the same 4,480
words.  Three hexadecimal values in that header are commented-out
alternatives and must not be treated as array initializers by extraction
tools.

histb-avsp.bin is the raw little-endian serialization of those 4,480
initializer words in source order.  A binary resource is used instead of
copying the giant array into the V4L2 driver because the standard Linux
firmware API consumes a byte image, keeps the SCD machine program separate
from host driver code, and permits the same checked image to be either
built into vmlinux or supplied by the root filesystem.  The retired VFMW
framework linked the array directly; it is not carried into the Request API
driver.

The HiSTB arm64 defconfigs embed the image through CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE.
The histb-vdec driver requests it as hisilicon/histb-avsp.bin and checks
the exact size before copying it to a 128-byte-aligned coherent buffer.
